{"kind":"report","text":"CHANGES-REQUESTED at exact clean head 804bcd1f9af3cc050733688d3f7186e466aacbb6, judging artifact c434cdd076e494426ff039b083809491089283a0.\n\nTwo blocking findings.\n\n1. The embedded UI was not rebuilt. The exact diff contains no `internal/service/uidist` change, and the committed bundle contains none of the new “commitments need attention” copy. Merging this head would leave the browser served by the Go binary on the old mixed-count behavior even though the TypeScript source is fixed. Run the normal UI generation, commit the replaced bundle and index, and make `make ui-check` green at the replacement head.\n\n2. The separately reported artifact count is not an artifact count. Both TopBar and WorkDrawer use `otherWorkAttention(projection).length`, but `otherWorkAttention` deliberately returns stale/retired artifacts plus unlinked promises. The changed test proves the mismatch with one artifact and one unlinked promise, then the UI would call the resulting value 2 artifacts. Preserve both signals, but count and label them honestly — for example separate artifact and unlinked-promise counts, or label the aggregate “other attention items” and expose its composition. Add a rendered/call-site regression so the existing mixed fixture cannot be mislabeled again.\n\nThe core arithmetic is otherwise correct: active, closed and lifecycle-stale partition the commitment population; attention remains an overlapping qualifier; all counts now come from the projection rather than topic membership. Changing the old test from a mixed total was the right decision and its explanation is unusually clear. `npm test --prefix ui` passes 71/71 and `npx tsc --noEmit` passes.","body":{"head":"804bcd1f9af3cc050733688d3f7186e466aacbb6","verdict":"changes-requested"}}